  The two engine/templates lines are not a contradiction. The markdown in the
  scaffold template is routing content: it carries the mandatory-routing
  paragraph, the tree shape, the priority-on-collision ordering, and the
  rationalization block. It is the routing most likely to be copied, because it
  exists to be copied, so it carries share-alike. The plugin config beside it
  executes, so it does not.

The split is deliberate. The engine is a mechanism and we want it adopted
everywhere, so it carries a permissive license with a patent grant. The routing
content is the work: which skill fires at which gate, in which order, with which
refusal. That is what took the time, and it carries share-alike so a commercial
derivative has to stay open on the same terms.

This split is enforced by a test, not just by this file:
engine/tests/license-coverage.test.ts fails if any file is not covered by
exactly one bucket.

ATTRIBUTION IS NOT OPTIONAL AND ITS FORM IS SPECIFIED.

CC BY-SA 4.0 section 3(a)(1) lets the licensor state the manner of attribution.
Flowy states it in ATTRIBUTION.md. Read that file before reusing any routing
content, commercially or otherwise. The short version: credit "Routing by Flowy",
link to https://www.flowy.sh with a live followable link on the surface where the
routing is used, name the license, link to the file you took, and say what you
changed. That credit string and that host are the same everywhere: this file,
ATTRIBUTION.md, README.md, the fork notice the hook prints, and the terms
published at https://www.flowy.sh/license.

ROUTED SKILLS ARE NOT OURS.

Every Flow in overlays/ routes to skills that live in separately installed,
independently licensed plugins. Nothing in this repository bundles them and
nothing here claims them. Each Flow's ATTRIBUTION.md names the upstream author,
license, and source for every skill it routes to. Upstream licenses govern
upstream skills. Our license covers only our routing.

